{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1092", "sample_idx": 28, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "My Japan Trip in 300 Words\n\nI arrived in Kyoto just as cherry blossoms began to fall—a soft pink snow dusting the cobblestones. I spent mornings wandering Kinkaku-ji, its golden pavilion shimmering over still water, and afternoons sipping matcha in quiet tea houses where silence felt sacred. In Nara, deer bowed politely for senbei, their eyes knowing, ancient.\n\nTokyo was a symphony of contrasts. Shibuya’s scramble crossing pulsed like a living organism; Shinjuku’s neon signs blinked in languages I didn’t understand but felt deeply. I ate ramen at a 10-seat stall in Golden Gai, where the chef didn’t speak English but smiled as he slid a bowl toward me—rich broth, perfect noodles, a single slice of chashu that melted like butter.\n\nI took the shinkansen to Hiroshima. The Peace Memorial Park was hushed, heavy with memory. A single origami crane, left by a child, sat on the edge of the monument. I folded another, whispering thanks—for peace, for presence.\n\nAt night, I wandered through Osaka’s Dotonbori, slurping takoyaki from a paper cone, laughing with strangers who offered me bites of their own. In a ryokan outside Hakone, I bathed in an onsen under a sky full of stars, steam curling around me like a secret.\n\nJapan didn’t just show me beauty—it taught me stillness. In the way a temple bell rings once, then lingers. In the precision of a folded napkin. In the quiet dignity of a stranger bowing as they passed.\n\nI left with a suitcase full of matcha, a journal full of sketches, and something heavier: a heart that learned to move slower, see deeper.\n\nI’ll be back. Not to see more—but to feel more.\n\n— 297 words", "logprobs": [{"token": "My", "logprob": -0.23346616327762604}, {"token": " Japan", "logprob": -0.2209511399269104}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": -0.09506416320800781}, {"token": " in", "logprob": -0.31332796812057495}, {"token"